  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 1021 PM CDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nSouthwesterly upper flow will continue to dominate the region \nthrough mid week as an upper high builds over the northern Gulf \nCoast. This is not to say the weather will be fully quiet. The broad \nupper low currently over British Columbia will push southward along \nthe West Coast, becoming more bowling ball-like with time. It will \nthen push west across the Central Rockies and enhance upper \ndiffluence over the Southern Plains as it moves east. Models are in \ndecent agreement with the potential for some showers and \nthunderstorms across the FA late Tuesday and possibly Wednesday. \nModels diverge greatly beyond mid week. The GFS is very progressive \nwith the upper low as it moves across the Central Plains. This is \nfollowed by a deep upper low, ~H537, across the Great Lakes that \npushes the first strong cold front through the region by late week, \nbringing H850 temps of 2-6 degrees C. The ECMWF does not allow the \nupper low to push further east than the Rockies and instead reforms \nit over the Baja Peninsula. It also brings a cold front into the FA \nalong with rain, but this front is much warmer as the airmass is \nhighly modified and, with upper ridging extending from the Central \nPlains well into Canada, keeps the coolest air well to our north.",
